{"at_ms":8161,"event":{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"ref-0003","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tide tables differ between ports because tides aren't uniform across coastlines. The moon and sun pull on all ocean water equally, but local geography dramatically reshapes this universal force.\n\nA port's tidal pattern depends on its coastal bathymetry—the seafloor's shape and depth. Shallow, funnel-shaped bays can amplify tidal ranges, while protected harbors may experience minimal movement. Continental shelves vary in width, affecting how tidal waves propagate.\n\nAmphidromes—points around which tides rotate—create regions of high and low tidal range depending on proximity. The Coriolis effect also plays a role, deflecting tidal currents differently by latitude, which alters local timing and height.\n\nDistance from the lunar equator influences each location's tidal range, as does the angle of approach for tidal waves reflecting off coastlines. Bays and estuaries can develop resonance patterns that amplify or dampen tidal movements.\n\nThese factors mean a port 50 miles away might experience tides hours apart or with dramatically different ranges—why mariners need specific tables for each location.","citations":[]}],"model":"anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5","stop_reason":null,"stop_details":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":664,"output_tokens":3,"output_tokens_details":null,"cache_creation_input_tokens":28772,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":28772,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":"standard","speed":"standard"},"provider":"Amazon Bedrock","context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ref-0001","uuid":"ref-0125","timestamp":"2026-10-15T05:21:47.011Z","request_id":"ref-0008"}}
