ity. The Coriolis effect also plays a role, deflecting tidal currents differently by latitude, which alters local timing and height.\n\nDistance from the lunar equator influences each location's tidal range, as does the angle of approach for tidal waves reflecting off coastlines. Bays and estuaries can develop resonance patterns that amplify or dampen tidal movements.\n\nThese factors mean a port 50 miles away might experience tides hours apart or with dramatically different ranges—why mariners need specific tables for each location.","ttft_ms":4257,"type":"result","duration_ms":7101,"uuid":"ref-0129","ttft_stream_ms":4255,"time_to_request_ms":144,"first_content_frame_ms":4255,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}}
